# `genesis/orchestrator/data` — deployment-record governance

`deployments.json` resource fields are a **class** property (deviceArchetype). This binding surfaces
intra-archetype drift (one human bumped, its class left behind) as an `ask`; realign the class by
moving the archetype budget, or declare a justified `resourceOverride` on the exceptional human.

The aggregate binding reads typed `cluster.ratifications` in `compute-capacity.json`: only CPU
limits may exceed allocatable up to the ratified percentage; requests reserve and memory is
incompressible. A ledger older than 30 days refers for refresh without an aggregate-capacity ask,
retaining the underlying violations in the message. Run `k8s-bridge observe` to promote a fresh
cluster observation; the command lands with Station 2/3b.

## A limit RAISE is an algedonic signal, not a setting

The two bindings above ask whether a value is COHERENT (with its class, with the portfolio).
The third asks a prior question: whether RAISING it is the right move at all. It fires only on a
raise — never on a lower or equal value, never on a new human, never on an unparseable pending edit
— and it refuses to be a questionnaire. It composes predicted demand from what this tree already
declares (the archetype canonical budget, a ratified `resourceOverride` read from the PRE-state, the
Rakia `limits.cpu_m` overcommit ratification, `edgenodeArcFactor`, the doorway manifests' declared
hosted cast) and prints **requested vs predicted with the ratio**, plus the count of prior raise
stamps this unit already carries.

Ashby allows either move — attenuate the disturbance, or amplify the regulator — so a raise is
never refused. What it is not allowed to be is *silent repetition*: at or above two prior stamps on
one unit the message reads BREACH rather than APPROACH and says a design pass is due first. That is
Meadows' shifting-the-burden-to-the-intervenor, and `$cpuBumpComment` / `$cpuThrottleBumpComment` /
`$memoryBumpComment` / `$cpuLimitBump` in this very file are the evidence it counts.

The demand model is a PROJECTION, named as one: `ark_core::manifest::Envelope` carries `bound`, never
`demand`, and `elohim_compute::ResourceSnapshot` observes bytes and documents, never CPU or memory.
The smallest native home to add is `Envelope::demand` beside `Envelope::bound` — which is also this
rule's retire condition.

The model is composed from what this tree declares, and each number names its source:

| fact | declared at |
|---|---|
| archetype canonical budget | `genesis/data/devices/archetype-resource-budgets.json` |
| ratified per-unit exception | `resourceOverride.{field}` + `justification`, read from the **pre**-state so a raise *of* the override is measured against the class, not against itself |
| overcommit tolerance | `genesis/data/rakia/compute-capacity.json` `cluster.ratifications[]` where `dimension: limits.cpu_m` — 125% today; memory carries no ratification and is incompressible, so 100% |
| conductor/storage split | `bridges/k8s::share()` — conductor 1/2 CPU and 5/8 memory, storage the remainder |
| authority arc | `edgenodeArcFactor` (absent = full arc) |
| hosted cast | `DOORWAY_MAX_AGENTS_PER_CONDUCTOR` in `../manifests/doorway/*.yaml` |
| recurrence | prior raise stamps on the unit, counted from the pre-state |

**BREACH is objective**, not rhetorical: requested > predicted x the ratified tolerance, **or** the
unit already carries at/over two prior raise stamps (measure `resource-limit-raises@1`, lens
`resource-limit-raise-recurrence` in `.claude/epr-meta/measures.yaml` — the validator reads that
watermark rather than hardcoding it). Anything else is APPROACH. The arc-scaling spec's ruling is
surfaced verbatim on a memory raise at full arc: *"no RAM bump reconciles that… the durable lever is
the arc, not the limit."*

The three questions the ask puts to the author, in order: (a) **what disturbance** is the limit
absorbing — name the loop and cite a diagnosis path that *resolves on disk*, by path or by backlog
slug, because an unresolvable citation is not a reference; (b) **which seam we own** attenuates it at
the source, routed through the concern-routing atlas, because a raise that names no seam is the
intervenor taking the load permanently; (c) **what is the restoration condition, and what would
falsify the raise** — *"the unit pins at the new limit with the symptom unchanged"* is the usual
falsifier, and it says the burn was never headroom. A stamp that already cites a resolving diagnosis
**and** carries a restoration condition is told so, so a considered raise reads differently from a
bare one — followed by the reminder that a citation is not an attenuation.

### Why this rule is declared inline instead of bound to a pinned policy

Its two siblings above bind pinned rows in `.claude/epr-meta/policies.yaml`. The registry's
former 65,536-byte limit no longer prevents this rule from joining them: both
`_lib/epr_meta.py` (`_MAX_REGISTRY_BYTES`) and `elohim/eprfs/eprfs-meta/src/lib.rs`
(`MAX_REGISTRY_BYTES`) now allow 1 MiB. The roughly 73 KB policy registry is well below that
limit. The earlier 99.1%-full rationale is obsolete.

The rule remains inline for this prose-only correction so its existing ratified terms and
scope remain unchanged. It does not technically need to live inline: moving this rule and
its duplicate in `../manifests/.epr-meta` into one pinned policy is a separate governance
change requiring a shared policy row and both bindings to be verified together. Until that
migration, the duplication is explicit maintenance debt, not protection against a size cap.

Consequence to expect, and it is correct: an inline `ask` with no ratified policy pin is referred
once by `epr:validator-escalation-ladder`. Under the agency charter, accepting the merge at the
`dev` rung *is* the ratification event.
